# Everything in this folder is read by a Linux container, so it must arrive with LF endings even on a Windows
# checkout (hosted-security-hardening Part 2).
#
# ⚠️ Why this file exists. The repository sets `core.autocrlf=true` and carries no other `.gitattributes`, so on
# Windows every script here lands in the working tree as CRLF. Git stores LF and normalises again on a Linux
# checkout, so an operator deploying from a clone is unaffected — but anyone who builds these images ON Windows
# gets `exec /usr/local/bin/backup.sh: no such file or directory`, because the shebang line ends `#!/bin/sh\r`
# and there is no such interpreter. The message names a file that plainly exists, which is why it costs a cycle
# every time somebody meets it.
#
# ⚠️ `pg_hba.conf` is the sharper case: PostgreSQL parses it token by token, so a trailing `\r` corrupts the
# authentication METHOD on every line — and that file is the whole of FR-2.3's cleartext refusal.
#
# Deliberately scoped to `deploy/` rather than added at the repository root: `packaging/` ships PowerShell and
# Inno Setup files that are read by Windows tools, and one of them (an `.iss` with French dialog strings) needs
# a BOM. Those have their own encoding rules and are not this file's business.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
Caddyfile   text eol=lf
